work That is our commute time

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.720
given the time we leave in the
morning

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.060
The first model that we studied,

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.260
the constant model,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.980
it looked like a flat line.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.620
And then what we discussed at
length over the last two

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.280
lectures was where is the
optimal place to put that flat

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:37.840
line?

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.020
And the answer that we've
discovered

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.880
is that it depends on which loss
function we choose.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.000
If we choose squared loss as our
loss function, then the optimal

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.880
place to place that constant
model

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.240
was at the average or mean of
the Y values.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.960
If we use absolute loss, Well,
then there's a different

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.580
location.
Then it's the median of the y

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.760
values.
And what you played around with

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.900
in lab yesterday was if you
think of these other loss

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.240
functions, well, then there are
other best places to put that

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.740
horizontal line.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.220
Now we are dealing with the
simple linear regression model,

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:16.640
which has the form h of xi
equals w0

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.560
plus w1 xi.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.060
Someone remind me what these two
things are called, W0 and W1.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.420
What's the name for those?

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:27.740
Kevin?

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:28.200
Parameters.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.700
Parameters, good.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.760
We have two parameters here.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.580
And the question is, well, how
do we pick those parameters so

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.600
that the resulting predictions
are as good as they can be?

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.740
We should do it in a more
principled way than just

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:46.920
eyeballing it.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:52.040
And that's where the three-step
modeling recipe comes back into

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.540
the picture.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.060
And what was the more formal
term for this three-step

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.060
modeling recipe that we
introduced on Tuesday?

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.700
Someone remind me.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:07.800
Started with an E,

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.800
sounded kind of fancy.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.150
I don't remember.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.060
What's up?

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.560
Empirical risk.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.160
Yeah, good.
Empirical risk.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.220
Minimization.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.880
That first step involves
choosing a model.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.180
We've now seen two.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:39.430
Today we are going to pick the
simple linear regression model.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.280
Can you remind me why it's
called simple linear regression?

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.560
I commented on this on Tuesday.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:55.840
Yes, and remind me of your name
again?

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:56.580
Zara.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.580
Yeah,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.180
there's only one input variable.
That's why we're calling it

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.120
simple.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:01.700
Here,

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.220
that input variable is denoted
xi.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:07.260
We know that that means the time
we leave in the morning or the

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.760
departure time.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.080
In the future, we will learn
about multiple regression.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.330
The next stage is to choose a
loss function.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.560
Once again, we've seen two of
these, but we're going to pick

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:26.060
the more common choice for now,

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.720
which is squared loss,

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.220
which returns

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:37.160
is the square difference between
an actual y value

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:42.050
and a predicted y value.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:44.960
So now,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.200
what would be wrong with me
saying that the third step is

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.180
minimizing the loss function?

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.980
What's wrong with that in terms
of the terminology we've

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.680
introduced here? Why would it be
wrong to say, I'm minimizing my

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:01.820
loss function?

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.040
That's my goal.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.660
Why is that wrong?

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:06.160
What's up?

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:07.260
Oh, sorry.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.260
You're up, what's up?

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:08.800
Maaj.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.900
I'm trying to minimize average
loss.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.180
Good, good, good.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.380
That's the key.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.420
Loss measures how close or far
one prediction is to its actual

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:18.420
y value.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:19.960
We don't care about minimizing
that.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.660
We care about minimizing average
loss across an entire data set.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:24.340
Remember,

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:28.140
one loss function only tells you
how close or far one prediction

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.200
is for one row of your data set.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.780
We care about predictions being
good on average across the whole

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:37.340
data set, which is why we
minimize

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:41.240
average loss

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:49.580
to

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:50.580
find

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.300
optimal parameters.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:00.320
And so one of the last things we
did on Tuesday is we wrote out

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.080
what the formula for average
loss looks like when we've

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.760
chosen the simple linear
regression model and the squared

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:09.260
loss-loss function.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.220
We had a 1 over n.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:16.060
Maybe I'll put this in red like
everything else.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:18.860
I had a 1 over n.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.040
I had a sum symbol,

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.380
which went from i equals 1 to n.
Any time we have average loss,

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:25.900
you will see this pattern.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.620
1 over n times the sum,

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.640
where i goes from 1 to n.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:36.280
The things I summed were yi
minus something squared.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.620
And that something,

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:42.260
I replace it with how I'm making
my predictions.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.340
That is the definition of my
model.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.140
And here, that definition is w0
plus w1

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:50.360
xi.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:59.240
And one of the things we
discussed

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.080
is that this is a function on
the right of how many input

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:04.080
variables?

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.600
How many input variables are
there for this function on the

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:09.600
right?

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.900
Two, w0 and w1.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:18.340
When before,

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:22.160
when we minimized average loss
and we were finding to find the

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.360
best W for the constant model,

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:28.600
the average loss function,
whether that was mean squared

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.060
error, or mean absolute error,

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:34.280
or mean 0, 1 loss, which was one
of the examples we talked about

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:35.320
on Tuesday,

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.900
all of those were functions of
just one input variable,

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.900
W, because we only had one
parameter.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.420
Here we have two parameters.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:46.170
So this function we are
minimizing

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.400
takes two input variables.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:54.140
So we're here, we're treating
the x's and the y's, that is our

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.260
data set, as being some
constant.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:00.060
As in it is like not a thing
that can change as a result of

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.560
anything that we do.
The data set is just provided to

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:03.560
us.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.480
So we're clear on what our goal
is.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.780
Our goal is to find a
combination of w0 and w1 that

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:15.030
make this function,

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.780
which we would also call mean
squared error, by the way,

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:27.160
but maybe we'd call it mean
squared error for the simple

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.440
linear regression model.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.440
SLR is just simple linear
regression.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:39.120
Our goal is to find the
combination of inputs that gives

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.700
us the lowest possible value
here.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:42.920
Why is that?

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:47.440
Well, it's because that
combination of w0 and w1

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.150
should give us the line that
makes

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:55.420
the best predictions on the data
set that we've collected.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:58.460
Are we clear on what our goal
is?

00:08:58.460 --> 00:08:59.460
OK.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.840
So at the end of Tuesday's
class,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.000
we wrote out this formula for
mean squared error for the

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.940
simple linear regression model.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.660
And then I just told you the
parameters that minimized it.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:21.500
But part of what I want to start
with today is understanding how

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.740
one would find those parameters.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:26.580
And to do that, we need to think
about what does this function

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.830
actually look like?

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:42.320
Q, what does this look

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:43.320
like?

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.710
So to refresh our memory,

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:53.360
when we were minimizing mean
squared error for the constant

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.860
model when we had one parameter,

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:07.380
what did the resulting graph
that we were minimizing look

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.180
like?

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:11.300
If one axis was w,

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.600
and the other axis was the
output of this function,

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.100
what did the graph look like?

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:21.760
Remind me.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.380
A parabola.
A parabola, good.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:32.000
And what we've studied at length
is the x-axis value

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.860
of

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.580
the vertex of the parabola.

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.100
It tells us what w to plug in to
get to the bottom of the

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:44.100
parabola.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:48.340
One of the things that I didn't
explicitly say in lecture, but

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:52.220
has maybe become clear by
working on homework one and the

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:58.140
recent labs, is that the
vertical component here, that is

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:00.320
what this is,

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:02.660
also has a familiar name.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.910
What is that?

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:09.660
Like when I plug in the mean of
the y's into this mean squared

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:11.040
error function,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.260
what is the resulting output
called?

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.460
It has a name that you've heard
of before.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:16.900
Yeah?

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:18.400
3 times.

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:20.080
Not quite.

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:24.580
I'm asking for the vertical
position of the vertex,

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:25.800
yes.
But I'm saying,

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:26.800
like,

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:30.560
that position is described in
terms of the data set, using a

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.060
familiar term.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.420
Do you know, Andrew?

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:37.420
Chinmay.

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:43.040
I'm looking for something that
is like a formula you can

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.840
calculate just given the y's.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:46.840
Average?

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:50.380
Not quite.
That's the input that minimizes

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:51.920
the function.
I'm asking, what is the

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:53.240
resulting output?

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:54.300
Ankita.
The variance.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:55.820
Good.
It's the variance of the y.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.600
Why is that?

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:04.600
Well,

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:10.250
look at what happens when I plug
in the average y here.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:15.860
Well, all this tells me then is
that this is the average squared

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:17.980
distance from the mean,

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:20.480
and that's the definition of the
variance.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:25.730
And actually, if we take a look
at homework one,

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:27.580
due tomorrow,

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:33.740
this is one of the big ideas.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:39.300
And actually,

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.540
we just answered one of the
questions, which is the last one

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:43.540
here,

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:45.820
part E.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:50.560
What you do there is you rewrite
mean squared error for the

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:52.060
constant model.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:58.680
Notice, this is not exactly the
formula that was on the screen

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:02.340
before.
Before, that was sum of yi minus

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:03.840
w squared.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.180
So what we're currently looking
at is a little different.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:10.040
What I'm saying is that the
formula I wrote before is equal

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:12.290
to this one,

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.980
where instead of summing yi
minus w squared,

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:18.580
it's the sum of yi minus y bar,

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:21.840
which is the average or mean of
the y's squared, plus this other

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:24.000
thing that involves the w's.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.260
This is, again,

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:26.780
something that will make more
sense once you work through this

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:27.780
question.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:30.100
But what we're getting at with
this question

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:36.100
is that this first piece is

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:40.200
the variance of the y's.
So a mean squared error actually

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:44.480
equals the variance of the y's
plus something that depends on

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:45.780
the w.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:49.060
But you can see, in order to
minimize this overall function,

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:52.440
well, this thing w has no
control over.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.680
It doesn't matter what w is,
this first term can't change.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:57.360
What w can,

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:00.020
where w does come into play is
the second term.

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:02.140
And if w equals y bar,

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:04.380
well, then that's how you make
this as small as possible, which

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:06.540
means that's how you minimize
that function.

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:11.040
But when w equals y bar,

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.260
well, then that matches what we
saw with the calculus-based

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.940
approach with y bar, or the
average y,

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:18.260
minimizing this function.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:21.220
But that means if you plug in w
equals y bar,

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:22.580
this piece goes away,

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:24.440
and then mean squared error is
equal to the variance of the

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:25.440
lies.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:29.060
So this is an important idea,

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:30.780
one that's talked about in the
notes, and one that I want you

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:32.580
to internalize.
OK?

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.240
That was sort of a side quest,

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:38.640
but one that was important for
us to talk about.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:43.800
Going back to where we are in
today's lecture,

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:47.380
when we were dealing with the
constant model, which had one

